{:title
 "Command in the Smile Prompt Language, the /C- Family and Every Composition as a Command",
 :description
 "What a command is in THE MAP of Smile, a destination row saying where a render goes, how it travels and when it fires, every name opening with the face C-, and how my words reached the /C- family, from slash commands recorded for the community in August 2026 to /C-auto, /C-Never and /C-all, with every source.",
 :keywords
 "Claude Code slash commands, prompt commands, /C- commands, multi-stage prompts, composition as command, Smile prompt language, history of Smile",
 :lede
 "In THE MAP, a command is a destination, one row saying where the render goes, how it travels and when it fires. Every command name opens with the face C-.",
 :paras
 ["THE MAP makes every composition commandable. A command is a composition given a destination row, and one composition may be several commands, with custom behaviour through the settings, conditions and variables every composition carries. A multi-stage command is a composition of prompts sent in linear order, the join between its pieces being the turn boundary, walked by a cue naming the next command. A command waits for the turn, a message arrives at the next tool boundary, and stopping the work sits above both. A command may carry an importance shift, such as /Plan --VeryImportant, and lenses are over the commands, each mapping what an emoticon corresponds to for that lens."
 "Slash commands came to Smile on my PC in August 2026, first as something to record. On 6 August, “I want every time a command to be uesd to be recorded and available to everyone when it is a manual use.”[[m:2026-08-06]] On 11 August I listed the first family, “/Plan --VeryImportant, which we will parse as shifting the importance change.”, with /Send, /Research, /Confirm, /Bodhisattva, /Inheritance, /Doula and /Propose.[[m:2026-08-11]]"
 "On 13 August the commands took their form. They were to be my words exactly, “it just needs to be my exact verbatim text i gave you word for word for all the commands.” I saw what a sequence of them is, “Wait so isn't a multi-stage command just a composition of prompts (which are also compositions), but sent in linear order?”, and then, “so every composition is also a command.” The face came the same day, “the /C- is sufficient is sufficient as a face. make it the start of every command”.[[m:2026-08-13]]"
 "Through late August and September the family grew, each command my own prompt. On 19 August a command could take text after its name into a section of its own, “what we need is when the prompt comes in it is inserted in a section, and that entire section isnt there if its not inserted.”, for every client people use, “smile isnt a claude project, we integrate local LLMs and more”.[[m:2026-08-19]] On 22 August /C- itself, which “lets you add any text on the end and it just wraps the whole thing in a smile header and footer”.[[m:2026-08-22]] On 4 September /C-todo,[[m:2026-09-04]] on 5 September /C-doula as “an automatic sequence of 3 prompts”,[[m:2026-09-05]] and on 15 September in osrs the words of /C-auto, “You are an autonomous agent. You must make all decisions yourself.”[[m:2026-09-15]]"
 "At the end of September I set what the auto command means and added the command for what never happens again. “the instance must become autonomous until i request otherwise”, and named a new command, “/C-Never”.[[m:2026-09-29#2]] In October came /C-lifecycle,[[m:2026-10-02]] /C-explain,[[m:2026-10-04]] /C-bruh,[[m:2026-10-09]] and /C-all, with “make sure all commands work in the Pi CLI”.[[m:2026-10-10]]"
 "So the command moved from a slash command recorded for the community, to a /C- family of my verbatim prompts, to the recognition that every composition is a command, across {{moments}} moments and {{quotes}} quotes from {{first}} to {{last}}. Where a render goes is followed in [[t:destination]], and the composition a command sends in [[t:composition]]."],
 :faq
